 "drivingQuestion": "How does the mishna's prohibition square with the baraita permitting sipping and swallowing?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Two competing resolutions, Abaye and Rava",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.111a.5",
   "daf": "111a",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "לֹא יְגַמֵּעַ בָּהֶן אֶת הַחוֹמֶץ. וְהָתַנְיָא: לֹא יְגַמֵּעַ וּפוֹלֵט, אֲבָל מְגַמֵּעַ וּבוֹלֵעַ! אֲמַר אַבָּיֵי: כִּי תְּנַן נָמֵי מַתְנִיתִין, מְגַמֵּעַ וּפוֹלֵט תְּנַן. רָבָא אָמַר: אֲפִילּוּ תֵּימָא מְגַמֵּעַ וּבוֹלֵעַ, כָּאן — לִפְנֵי טִיבּוּל, כָּאן — לְאַחַר טִיבּוּל.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: «One may not sip vinegar through» his teeth on Shabbat. The Gemara asks: «Wasn’t it taught» in a baraita that the prohibition to sip vinegar on Shabbat means that «one may not sip and spit» it «out; however, one may sip and swallow» it? «Abaye said: When we learned» this ruling in «the mishna, we learned» it with regard to the case of «one who sips and spits» it «out. Rava said: Even if you say» that the mishna prohibits sipping vinegar even in a case where «one sips and swallows» it, there is still a distinction. «Here,» the baraita permits sipping vinegar «before dipping» one’s food in it, as he appears to be doing so for pleasure. «Here,» the mishna prohibits sipping vinegar «after dipping,» when it is clear that one is doing so for medicinal purposes."
  }
 ],
